    content: "Deuteronomy 18:20-22 establishes one of the earliest and most direct tests for prophetic authenticity: fulfillment. If a prophet speaks in God's name and the predicted event does not occur, the word was not from God. Moses attaches the death penalty to presumptuous prophecy — a severity that reflects the gravity of speaking falsely in God's name. The test is not designed to create paranoia but to protect the community from manipulation. It also implies that prophetic claims are verifiable; they are not mystical utterances exempt from examination. God invites scrutiny of those who claim to speak for Him.\n\nJesus' warning in Matthew 7:15-20 shifts the diagnostic from prediction to character. False prophets are dangerous precisely because they look convincing — they arrive in sheep's clothing, not wolf's fur. Their deception is not always in what they say but in what they are. Jesus says the reliable indicator is fruit: the long-term moral, relational, and spiritual consequences of a prophet's ministry. Does the teaching produce humility or arrogance? Generosity or exploitation? Deeper engagement with God or dependence on the prophet? The fruit test requires patience and observation, which is why Jesus frames it with agricultural imagery — you have to wait for the harvest to evaluate the tree.\n\nRevelation 22:18-19 addresses a different but related concern. John's warning about adding to or taking from \"the words of the prophecy of this book\" applies specifically to the book of Revelation, but it articulates a principle that governs the entire canon. The completed Scripture is not an open document awaiting supplementary chapters. The canon is closed — not because God has stopped communicating, but because the foundational, authoritative, universally binding revelation has been given in full. Any prophetic gift exercised after the canon's closure operates within the authority of Scripture, not alongside it or above it.\n\nThree misunderstandings commonly arise from these texts. First, some conclude that because the canon is closed, God no longer gives the prophetic gift at all — a conclusion the texts do not support. Deuteronomy 18 assumes prophets will continue to arise; the issue is how to test them, not whether to expect them. Second, some use the fruit test subjectively, declaring any prophet whose message they dislike to be a false prophet. Jesus' test is about sustained moral trajectory, not personal preference. Third, some read Revelation 22:18-19 as a prohibition against any prophetic activity whatsoever, which would contradict Paul's explicit instruction that the church should eagerly desire the gift of prophecy (1 Corinthians 14:1).",
    sortOrder: 2,
